our views about vaccines have become  blurred by  politics and anxiety by questions over  supply and safety  today the astrazeneca jab was once again  approved for use by europe's medical  regulator  which said the benefits outweigh any  potential link to a very rare type of  blood clot  its benefits in protecting people from  covet 19  with the associated risks of death and  hospitalization  outweigh the possible risks the  committee  also concluded that the vaccine is not  associated with an increase in the  overall risk of thromboembolic events or  blood clots  but despite this announcement on  continental europe the reputation of the  astrazeneca vaccine has been  bruised by questions over its efficacy  at this center in paris they're trying  to accelerate the vaccination process  amid a blend of  cynicism and skepticism  muriel's here she cheerfully took the  astrazeneca jab a few days ago  but she says that has led to a family  row i have a  twin who was supposed to come with me to  have  the first vaccine of astrazeneca  and he said i don't want to have it i  won't  fight her because astrazeneca is  it's not clean the european union  remains furious that it hasn't received  the doses of astrazeneca that it was  promised and ordered  today it started legal action against  the company  well there are plenty of theories to  explain europe's  sluggish vaccine rollout you can blame  bureaucracy  or top-heavy politics a rigid adherence  to the  two-dose plan but there are others who  say this is down to a supply chain  that europe by contrast to the uk or the  us has not done a good enough job  of securing the doses it needs  and that is a sentiment echo by one of  the city's leading public health experts  who told me he's frustrated by a lack of  vaccines  we don't have the dose of the vaccine  and also because it's the  the proudness of friends you know the  brother of friends  we see the uk the the us israel  and trying to get out the crisis and we  are still blocked  the vaccines are the solution to this  pandemic but we're not there yet  and europe trails the uk tonight  confronted by  a third wave of infections france  announced  it is tightening its lock down rules  again  adam parsons sky news paris
 

